{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## LIVE VALIDATION — MET. First-hand evidence against the deployed endpoint (supersedes comment 2)\n\n**This comment SUPERSEDES the earlier comment titled \"Live-validation criterion — what was proved,\nand what is still outstanding,\" which said \"I am NOT ticking the live-validation box\" and explained\nthat the endpoint \"cannot confirm the fix until the change lands on `main` and that host is\nredeployed.\"** That comment was accurate WHEN WRITTEN — the fix was then an uncommitted local\nworktree change. It is now stale in exactly the way it predicted would be resolved: the fix landed\non `main` as commit `bdfb9b3115c5ad29a685e04e71f5dce0e2ac21cc` (\"Emit JS-safe integers on the MCP\nwire\"), the host redeployed, and the calls below were made against that redeployed build. Read the\npresent comment, not that one, for the state of the live-validation criteria.\n\n### How this evidence was obtained\n\nBoth calls named in the criteria were issued **first-hand, just now**, from this session's\nconfigured MCP client against the DEPLOYED server at `https://rebar.solutions.navateam.com/mcp`\n(OAuth-authenticated through the already-configured client entry; no credential is reproduced\nhere). This is a live call to the deployed container, not a local `python -m rebar.mcp_server`\nprocess, not a replay, and not a simulation.\n\nThe client parses JSON **losslessly** — it is the class of client the preamble requires, the one\nwhose parse produced the originally reported `TypeError: Do not know how to serialize a BigInt`. A\nplain-`JSON.parse` client would have reported \"no error\" even against the broken build, so its\nsilence would prove nothing; that is why each assertion below is about the VALUE, not merely about\nthe absence of a throw.\n\n### Call 1 — `list_tickets({\"status\": \"open\", \"ticket_type\": \"epic\"})`\n\nThe exact call from the original bug report. **Result: SUCCESS.** A well-formed ticket list came\nback with no BigInt serialization error. Verbatim from the first row of the response\n(`alias: acute-agitable-xiphosuran`):\n\n```\n\"created_at\": \"1786649271159032001\"\n\"updated_at\": \"1787174253995137001\"\n```\n\nBoth arrive as JSON **strings** of decimal digits — the fixed wire form — not as bare 19-digit\nnumbers.\n\n### Call 2 — `ready_tickets({})`\n\n**Result: SUCCESS.** 939,719 characters of valid JSON, 76 ticket rows, no BigInt serialization\nerror. (This is the call that previously emitted 144 out-of-range bare integers.)\n\n### Machine-checked assertions over the full `ready_tickets` payload\n\nWalking every value in the complete response:\n\n```\nready_tickets rows: 76\nBARE integers outside JS-safe range:                   0\ndecimal-STRING ns timestamps outside JS-safe range:  235\n\n  .result[].created_at                             76\n  .result[].updated_at                             76\n  .result[].comments[].timestamp                   62\n  .result[].attestations.plan-review.signed_at     12\n  .result[].signature.signed_at                     7\n  .result[].attestations.mcp-exercise-step-1.signed_at   1\n  .result[].last_reopened_at                        1\n```\n\n**Zero** bare integers outside `[-(2**53)+1, (2**53)-1]` reach the wire. All 235 out-of-range ns\ntimestamps arrive as exact decimal strings — including the nested `list[dict]` carriers the root\ncause named (`comments[].timestamp`, `attestations.<kind>.signed_at`, `signature.signed_at`), which\nis the part that would have been missed by a fix applied only to declared model fields.\n\n### Exact round-trip, which is what makes \"no error\" mean something\n\n```\nint(\"1786649271159032001\") == 1786649271159032001      # re-stringifies identically: True\nfloat64 (what Number() / plain JSON.parse would give) -> 1786649271159032064\n```\n\nThe stored instant survives the round trip losslessly. The float64 column is the silent corruption\nthe truncating client was suffering: it differs from the true value in the last three digits, with\nno error raised. That contrast is the whole point of requiring a lossless client as the oracle.\n\n### Which build served this\n\nThe response came from the redeployed container serving `main` at or after\n`6fe5c0d54574` — which contains `bdfb9b3115c5` (verified: `git merge-base --is-ancestor\nbdfb9b3115c5 6fe5c0d54574` exits 0). The redeploy postdates the merge, so this is the fixed build\nand not a coincidental pass. An operator observation on container `rebar-mcp-6fe5c0d54574-8093`\nindependently recorded the same shape earlier today via `show_ticket` (`created_at` and\n`updated_at` both `str`, no bare integers beyond the JS safe range, and\n`structuredContent.created_at` also `str` — confirming the two halves of the response agree, which\nwas the `model_validate` coercion path).\n\n**All three LIVE VALIDATION criteria are therefore met on first-hand deployed evidence, and the\nboxes are ticked accordingly.**\n"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787892705975347001,"uuid":"953ae33b-3862-4174-9363-db424b380917"}